The `alarm()` handler never runs because nothing calls `setAlarm()`. The hourly flush currently depends on `setTimeout`, which doesn't survive the object being evicted. I couldn't fetch the linked Cloudflare docs in this session, so the retry details below come from what I already know about alarms and weren't checked against the current pages.

## Problems

1. **The alarm is never scheduled.** No code calls `this.ctx.storage.setAlarm()`, so `alarm()` never fires.
2. **`setTimeout` doesn't last.** An idle Durable Object is evicted after tens of seconds, and its in-memory timers go with it, so a one-hour timer basically never fires. If the object does stay up, each request adds another timer, so N requests cause N flushes.
3. **Failures aren't retried.** `fetch` doesn't throw on 4xx or 5xx responses, so `flush()` finishes normally even when the sink rejected the batch. Alarms are retried with backoff only when `alarm()` throws.
4. **Data can be sent twice or lost.**
   - `pending` is never cleared after a flush, so the same items are sent every time.
   - Nothing writes to `pending`, so the body is the string `null`.
   - Other requests can run while the outgoing `fetch` is in progress. If you delete all of `pending` afterwards, items added during that window are lost.
5. **The alarm can run more than once.** A run can repeat, for example when it fails after the sink already received the batch. The sink needs a batch ID so it can drop duplicates.
6. **Calling `setAlarm()` on every request would postpone the flush indefinitely.** It replaces the existing alarm, so busy traffic keeps pushing it back. Only set it when no alarm is already scheduled.

## Suggested version

```ts
import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers";

const FLUSH_MS = 60 * 60 * 1000;

export class Room extends DurableObject {
  async fetch(request: Request) {
    const count = ((await this.ctx.storage.get<number>("count")) ?? 0) + 1;
    const pending = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    pending.push(await request.text());
    // No await between the two puts, so they commit together.
    this.ctx.storage.put({ count, pending });

    if ((await this.ctx.storage.getAlarm()) === null) {
      await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + FLUSH_MS);
    }
    return new Response(String(count));
  }

  async alarm() {
    const pending = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    if (pending.length === 0) return;

    const batchId = `${this.ctx.id}:${pending.length}:${await this.ctx.storage.get<number>("count")}`;
    const res = await fetch("https://example.com/sink", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Idempotency-Key": batchId },
      body: JSON.stringify(pending),
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`sink returned ${res.status}`); // triggers alarm retry

    // Remove only what was sent. Items appended during the fetch stay queued.
    const now = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    const rest = now.slice(pending.length);
    await this.ctx.storage.put("pending", rest);
    if (rest.length > 0) await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + FLUSH_MS);
  }
}
```

Two other changes I'd suggest:
- **Use SQLite for the queue.** Rewriting the whole `pending` array on every request gets expensive as the queue grows. A SQLite table with an autoincrement ID, deleted by `id <= lastSentId` after a flush, avoids that.
- **Use RPC methods.** A method like `stub.record(item)` replaces the `fetch` handler, as the skill recommends.

I read the Durable Objects skill and found four main problems. Nothing schedules the alarm, `setTimeout` timers are lost when the object is evicted, failed sink responses don't make the alarm retry, and `pending` is never cleared safely. The suggested version above fixes them by scheduling with `setAlarm` only when none is set, throwing when the sink fails, sending an idempotency key, and removing only the items that were sent.

